  "problem": "The particle moves along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into our expression for ${y}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot {(\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+3)^{2}})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{({t}^{2}+3)^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{t({t}^{2}+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
